  <p>
    Most men losing hair have male pattern hair loss. It is inherited, driven by the effect of
    DHT on genetically sensitive follicles, and it progresses gradually — the hairline recedes,
    the crown thins, and hair on the affected areas grows back finer and shorter each cycle
    until it stops growing at all.
  </p>

  <p>
    The single most useful thing to understand is that <strong>treatment protects what you still
    have far more reliably than it brings back what is gone.</strong> That is why the timing of
    the first consultation matters more than the choice of treatment.
  </p>

  <h2>What is actually causing it</h2>

  <p>
    Not all hair loss is pattern hair loss, and the difference changes the treatment completely.
    A consultation covers how long it has been happening, whether it is shedding or thinning,
    the pattern on the scalp, family history, and any recent illness, medication or weight change.
    A scalp examination and, often, blood tests for iron, vitamin D and thyroid function complete
    the picture.
  </p>

  <p>
    Sudden diffuse shedding two or three months after an illness, surgery, crash diet or major
    stress is usually telogen effluvium, which recovers on its own once the trigger resolves.
    Treating that as pattern hair loss wastes months.
  </p>

  <h2>Treatments that have real evidence</h2>

  <h3>Topical minoxidil</h3>
  <p>
    Applied to the scalp daily. It extends the growth phase and improves density. It is available
    without prescription, it is safe for long-term use, and shedding in the first few weeks is
    expected rather than a sign it is failing.
  </p>

  <h3>Oral anti-androgens</h3>
  <p>
    Finasteride, and less commonly dutasteride, reduce DHT and are the most effective medical
    treatment for male pattern hair loss. They also carry side effects that deserve a proper
    conversation before starting rather than after.
    <a href="/conditions/finasteride-dutasteride-side-effects/">We have written about that separately and in detail.</a>
  </p>

  <h3>PRP and GFC</h3>
  <p>
    Growth factors from your own blood, injected into the scalp across a course of sessions.
    Useful as an addition to medical treatment, particularly where thinning is diffuse.
  </p>

  <h3>Hair transplant</h3>
  <p>
    Moves permanent follicles from the back of the scalp to thinned areas. It is the only option
    that adds density where follicles are gone. It does not stop ongoing loss elsewhere, so
    medical treatment usually continues alongside it.
  </p>

  <h2>What does not work</h2>

  <p>
    Most shampoos, oils and supplement combinations sold for hair fall have no evidence behind
    them. Where iron or vitamin D is genuinely low, correcting it helps — but that is worth
    testing rather than guessing, and taking supplements you do not need does nothing.
  </p>
